        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a faithful, additive extension of the patterns already established in this codebase:\n\n- **Exception placement & shape**: `DailyOwnerLimitReachedException` lives in `rest.advice` alongside `OwnerCityAtCapacityException`, `DuplicateOwnerTelephoneException`, etc., extends `RuntimeException`, and carries the relevant data (`LocalDate date`) just as the siblings carry `city`/`telephone`. The `transient` on the field is a sensible touch consistent with it being a non-serializable payload.\n- **Handler**: `handleDailyOwnerLimitReachedException` mirrors the other `@ExceptionHandler` methods exactly \u2014 same `logger.debug` shape, `detailBuild` call, and a dedicated `ERROR_DAILY_LIMIT_REACHED` constant grouped with the other message constants. Omitting a `setProperty(\"errors\", \u2026)` is appropriate here since the limit is not tied to a specific input field.\n- **Controller guard**: `rejectDailyLimitReached()` matches the existing `reject*` method family (`rejectCityAtCapacity`, `rejectDuplicateTelephone`, `rejectDuplicateHousehold`) in naming, Javadoc style, and the `findAllOwners().stream().filter(...).count()` counting idiom used throughout this class. The `DAILY_OWNER_LIMIT` constant sits next to `CITY_CAPACITY` with parallel documentation, and the call is placed sensibly as an early fail-fast guard in `addOwner`.\n- Inlining the count rather than extracting a `countOwnersRegisteredToday()` helper is the right call \u2014 unlike `countOwnersInCity`, this count has only one caller, so there's no reuse pressure.\n\nLogic is in the right unit, nothing is duplicated, and no class is becoming a catch-all. A maintainer of this codebase would find this entirely natural."
      }
